# Weekly retro ritual

First analyze the requested period using commits and project evidence. Then prepare a concise status summary for {{ project_name }} covering product progress, launch and onboarding learnings, traction signals, major decisions, blockers, and next priorities. Highlight material changes and separate evidence from open questions.

Write for a non-technical, minimally versed reader: a spouse, investor, or advisor who understands the business but does not know our implementation history.

When the authoring agent has only been close to implementation work, narrow the scope accordingly. Product progress, launch posture, and onboarding learnings are usually enough; marketing can handle traction signals when it has the better context.

## Rules

- Constrain the relevance window to the dates requested by the user. Do not include older work just because it is salient or recently discussed.
- Verify the window before writing. Use `git log --since=<date>` and, when relevant, Linear issue history or project state.
- Keep the output short: roughly three-quarters of a page. Apply a high relevance threshold: include only changes that materially affect launch posture, product correctness, measurement quality, current risk, or the next decisions the project needs to make.
- Use only one-sentence bullets; each bullet should make the whole point sharply, without a follow-on explanation paragraph.
- Avoid implementation archaeology, project-code references, and tool names unless they materially change the business read.
- The document heading should be only the filing date, e.g. `# September 25, 2026`.
- Open with `## Operational fixes` when the week was primarily implementation / launch-hardening work.
- Prefer concise bold bullets, e.g. `- **The onboarding flow now tests whether visitors complete the first step.**`

- Distinguish:
  - **Evidence**: commits, deployed behavior, tested user flows, observed failures, explicit decisions.
  - **Open questions**: plausible interpretations, risks, and next things to learn.
- Include a short `## Glossary` at the end when jargon is unavoidable. Link the first occurrence of those terms to the glossary using same-document Obsidian links, e.g. `[[#ECS|ECS]]`, and format entries as `- **term** definition`.
- Prefer status and implications over exhaustive chronology. Avoid listing every shipped feature, config correction, or PR unless it changes how we launch, measure, operate, or de-risk the product.
- If the user asks for a draft first, provide the concise answer in chat before writing the vault document.

## File Pattern

Write a dated retro for each requested run. Do not schedule recurring work unless requested.

Write dated retros under:

```text
docs.vault/10-19 operations/14 retros/14.01 retros/
```

Use the next tertiary number:

```text
14.01.NN YYYY-MM-DD.md
```

Include YAML frontmatter with a `description` field.

## Maintenance

After adding or renaming a vault document, run:

```bash
python3 scripts/generate-vault-index.py
python3 scripts/generate-claude-md.py
```

Do not fold unrelated working tree changes into the retro branch.
